{title2=Stochastic Differential Equations}

= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Write the <stochastic differential equation> in integral form and replace the unknown integrand by the preceding <Picard iterate>. With the deterministic initial vector $a$, the recursion is
$$
\boxed{y_t^{(0)}=a,\qquad y_t^{(m+1)}=a+\sum_{i=1}^d\int_0^t A^iy_s^{(m)}\,dB_s^i\quad(m\geq0).}
$$
Each iterate is a continuous adapted process. The <Itô isometry> inductively gives its square integrability on every bounded time interval, so all these <Itô integrals> are well defined.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Use $s$ for the dummy integration variable in the printed integral. The <Itô isometry> gives
$$
\mathbb E|z_t^{(n+1)}|^2=C\int_0^t\mathbb E|z_s^{(n)}|^2\,ds
\leq\frac{C^{n+1}}{n!}\int_0^t s^n\,ds
=\boxed{\frac{C^{n+1}t^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.}
$$
The assumed second-moment bound also proves the integrability needed to apply the <Itô isometry>. The extra time integration is the source of the factorial improvement.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Set $K=\sum_i\|A^i\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2$, where the norms are <operator norms>, and $D_m=y^{(m+1)}-y^{(m)}$. Independence of the Brownian coordinates and the vector <Itô isometry> give
$$
\mathbb E|D_0(t)|^2\leq |a|^2Kt,\qquad
\mathbb E|D_m(t)|^2\leq K\int_0^t\mathbb E|D_{m-1}(s)|^2ds.
$$
Induction, as in part (b), yields the <factorial Picard estimate for a linear stochastic differential equation>
$$
\mathbb E|D_m(t)|^2\leq |a|^2\frac{(Kt)^{m+1}}{(m+1)!}.
$$
For $t\leq T$, the <triangle inequality> in $L^2$ therefore bounds any tail of the iterates by the corresponding tail of
$$
|a|\sum_{m\geq0}\frac{(KT)^{(m+1)/2}}{\sqrt{(m+1)!}}.
$$
This numerical series converges by the <ratio test>: successive terms have ratio $\sqrt{KT/(m+2)}\to0$. Thus the iterates are Cauchy in $L^2$, uniformly in the deterministic time parameter on each bounded interval. <Completeness of Lp spaces> gives a limit $y_t$. Once its continuous adapted version is chosen in part (d), the <Itô isometry> permits passage to the limit in the integral equation: the squared norm of each integral difference is bounded by $K\int_0^T\mathbb E|y_s^{(m)}-y_s|^2ds\to0$. Consequently **the Picard limit solves the stochastic differential equation**.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

For every $m$, $D_m$ is a continuous square-integrable vector <martingale>. Its norm is a <submartingale>, so the <Doob L2 maximal inequality> and the estimate from part (c) give
$$
\mathbb E\sup_{t\leq T}|D_m(t)|^2\leq4|a|^2\frac{(KT)^{m+1}}{(m+1)!}.
$$
The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> now shows
$$
\sum_m\mathbb E\sup_{t\leq T}|D_m(t)|
\leq2|a|\sum_m\frac{(KT)^{(m+1)/2}}{\sqrt{(m+1)!}}<\infty.
$$
By <Tonelli's theorem>, the random sum of these suprema is finite almost surely. Therefore the telescoping series $a+\sum_mD_m(t)$ converges absolutely and uniformly on $[0,T]$ outside one null event. Its limit is continuous and agrees at each fixed time with the $L^2$ limit from part (c). Taking the intersection of the probability-one events for integer $T$ provides a single version on the whole half-line. Hence
$$
\boxed{\sup_{t\leq T}|y_t^{(n)}-y_t^{(\infty)}|\longrightarrow0\quad\text{almost surely for every finite }T.}
$$
This also gives the requested pointwise almost-sure convergence of the full sequence.

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Standard <Brownian motion> is centered, so $m_t=0$. If $s\leq t$, write $B_t=B_s+(B_t-B_s)$; the final increment is independent of $B_s$ and has mean zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{m_t=0,\qquad\phi_{st}=\mathbb E(B_sB_t)=\min(s,t).}
$$
This is the <Brownian covariance kernel>. Together with joint Gaussianity and continuous paths, it gives the <Gaussian-process characterization of Brownian motion>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For positive times the transformed process is centered and jointly Gaussian, and
$$
\mathbb E(X_sX_t)=st\min(1/s,1/t)=\min(s,t).
$$
Its paths are continuous away from zero. To verify continuity at zero, we need $B_u/u\to0$ almost surely as $u\to\infty$. The <strong law of large numbers> applied to the independent increments $B_{n+1}-B_n$ gives $B_n/n\to0$. On the unit interval after $n$, the <Doob L2 maximal inequality> gives
$$
\mathbb P\left(\sup_{0\leq v\leq1}|B_{n+v}-B_n|>\varepsilon n\right)\leq\frac4{\varepsilon^2n^2}.
$$
The <Borel-Cantelli first lemma>, first for each positive rational $\varepsilon$, shows that these between-integer fluctuations divided by $n$ tend to zero. Hence $B_u/u\to0$ for all real $u\to\infty$, and $tB_{1/t}\to0$ as $t\downarrow0$.

The <Gaussian-process characterization of Brownian motion> now proves **$X$ is standard Brownian motion**, with respect to its natural filtration. This is <time inversion of Brownian motion>; the transformed process need not be adapted to the original forward filtration.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <Itô formula> for $f(x)=x^4$ gives
$$
d(B_t^4)=4B_t^3dB_t+6B_t^2dt.
$$
The stochastic integral has expectation zero: its square integrability on a finite horizon follows from the finite sixth moments of a <normal distribution>. Since $\mathbb EB_s^2=s$, integration and expectation yield
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EB_t^4=6\int_0^t s\,ds=3t^2.}
$$

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}
{c}

Put $Q_t=\langle M,M\rangle_t$ and $F(x,q)=e^{\sigma x-\sigma^2q/2}$. In the two-variable <Itô formula>, $Q$ has finite variation, while the only nonzero quadratic term is $d\langle M\rangle=dQ$. Therefore
$$
dF(M_t,Q_t)=\sigma F\,dM_t-\tfrac12\sigma^2F\,dQ_t
+\tfrac12\sigma^2F\,dQ_t=\sigma F\,dM_t.
$$
This proves the <stochastic exponential> solves the equation. Its initial value is $e^{\sigma M_0}$; no normalization $M_0=0$ was stipulated.

The resulting process is initially a <continuous local martingale>. Since $Q_t\geq0$ and $|M_t|<C$,
$$
0<Z_t=e^{\sigma M_t-\sigma^2Q_t/2}\leq e^{|\sigma|C}.
$$
A bounded local martingale is a true <martingale>: apply the martingale identity to a localizing sequence and use <dominated convergence> in conditional expectations. The deterministic bound works on every finite horizon, proving **$Z$ is a martingale on every $[0,T]$**. The bound on $Q$ also gives a positive deterministic lower bound $e^{-|\sigma|C-\sigma^2C/2}$, although the upper bound already proves the martingale assertion.

= B
