{title2=Brownian Motion in Complex Analysis}

= 4
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

There is a printed domain error: $\operatorname{Re}z>0$ defines the right half-plane. Taken literally, continuity forces $\operatorname{Re}Z_{T_H}=0$ almost surely, so its law is a point mass at zero. The displayed density belongs to the intended upper half-plane $\operatorname{Im}z>0$, with $b>0$. We prove that intended assertion explicitly.

Write $Z_t=a+X_t+i(b+Y_t)$ for independent standard <Brownian motions>. The exit time $T$ is the first time $Y$ hits $-b$, and depends only on $Y$. Its density, by the supplied <Brownian first-passage time> law, is $b(2\pi t^3)^{-1/2}e^{-b^2/(2t)}$. Conditional on $T=t$, the real exit coordinate is <normally distributed> with mean $a$ and variance $t$. Independence therefore gives
$$
h(x)=\frac b{2\pi}\int_0^\infty t^{-2}
\exp\left(-\frac{b^2+(x-a)^2}{2t}\right)dt.
$$
With $u=[b^2+(x-a)^2]/(2t)$, the integral is $2/[b^2+(x-a)^2]$. Thus the <Cauchy exit law from a Brownian half-plane> is
$$
\boxed{h(x)=\frac b{\pi[b^2+(x-a)^2]}.}
$$
For the literal right half-plane, the corresponding nondegenerate coordinate is $\operatorname{Im}Z_{T_H}$, whose density is $a/\{\pi[a^2+(y-b)^2]\}$ for $a>0$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

If $f:D\to D'$ is a <conformal map>, the image of planar <Brownian motion> in $D$ is Brownian motion in $D'$ after the increasing clock change
$$
A_t=\int_0^t|f'(Z_s)|^2ds.
$$
More precisely, $f(Z_{A^{-1}(u)})$ is planar Brownian motion, starting at $f(Z_0)$ and run up to the relevant exit time. The <Itô formula> and the <Cauchy-Riemann equations> give image-coordinate local martingales with common bracket $A$ and zero cross bracket; the <Lévy characterization of multidimensional Brownian motion> identifies the time-changed process. In particular, <conformal invariance of planar Brownian motion> transports exit locations and <harmonic measure>, while changing traversal speed.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $z=x+iy$ in the strip,
$$
f(z)=\cosh(\pi x/a)\cos(\pi y/a)
+i\sinh(\pi x/a)\sin(\pi y/a),
$$
so $\operatorname{Im}f(z)>0$. This is a bijective <conformal map> onto the upper half-plane. One way to verify bijectivity is to factor it as $w=e^{\pi z/a}$, followed by $(w+w^{-1})/2$. The first map gives $\operatorname{Im}w>0$, $|w|>1$. The two inverse roots of the second map have product one; for any point in the upper half-plane exactly one root has modulus greater than one, and its imaginary part is positive because $\operatorname{Im}[(w+w^{-1})/2]=(1-|w|^{-2})\operatorname{Im}w/2$.

The three boundary pieces map as follows: the lower edge goes to $(1,\infty)$, the upper edge to $(-\infty,-1)$, and the vertical edge to $(-1,1)$. Thus the desired exit event becomes exit from the upper half-plane to the left of $-1$. Write
$$
A=\cosh(\pi p/a)\cos(\pi q/a),\qquad
B=\sinh(\pi p/a)\sin(\pi q/a)>0.
$$
By <conformal invariance of harmonic measure> and the <Cauchy exit law from a Brownian half-plane>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\operatorname{Im}Z_{T_S}=a)
=\int_{-\infty}^{-1}\frac B{\pi[B^2+(x-A)^2]}dx
=\frac12-\frac1\pi\arctan\frac{1+A}{B}.}
$$
The strip exit is finite almost surely, since its imaginary coordinate exits $(0,a)$ in finite time; corners have probability zero because individual points are <polar points for planar Brownian motion>. This is the <top-edge exit probability of a Brownian half-strip>.

\Image[2010-half-strip-exit.png]
{title=Boundary pieces of the half-strip under the hyperbolic-cosine map}

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Let $\alpha=\pi q/a\in(0,\pi)$. With $q$ and $a$ fixed,
$$
\frac{1+A}{B}\longrightarrow\frac{\cos\alpha}{\sin\alpha}=\cot\alpha.
$$
The principal <arctangent> satisfies $\arctan(\cot\alpha)=\pi/2-\alpha$ throughout this interval. Substitution gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{p\to\infty}\mathbb P(\operatorname{Im}Z_{T_S}=a)=q/a.}
$$
Far from the vertical boundary, the limit agrees with the one-dimensional <Brownian motion> probability of reaching height $a$ before zero.

= 5
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The disk exit time is finite almost surely; for example, either coordinate of the planar <Brownian motion> eventually leaves $(-1,1)$. By path continuity, the exit lies on the unit circle. Starting at the origin, rotational invariance of <planar Brownian motion> makes the exit law invariant under every circle rotation. The unique rotation-invariant probability measure is normalized <arc length>. Consequently **$Z_{T_D}$ is uniform on the unit circle**, or equivalently its angle has density $1/(2\pi)$ on $[0,2\pi)$.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

By <conformal invariance of planar Brownian motion>, $w(Z_{T_D})$ has the uniform boundary law from part (a), because the image process starts at zero. A <disk automorphism> extends to an orientation-preserving boundary diffeomorphism. Choose a continuous angular lift $\phi:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfying
$$
w(e^{i\theta})=e^{i\phi(\theta)},\qquad
\phi(\theta+2\pi)=\phi(\theta)+2\pi,\qquad\phi'(\theta)>0.
$$
The principal-angle version may have a branch jump, which is harmless for the density. The <change of variables for probability densities> yields the exit-angle density $\phi'(\theta)/(2\pi)$. Differentiating the boundary relation gives
$$
i e^{i\theta}w'(e^{i\theta})=i\phi'(\theta)e^{i\phi(\theta)}.
$$
Taking absolute values proves $\phi'(\theta)=|w'(e^{i\theta})|$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P^{z_0}(\theta_{T_D}\in d\theta)=\frac{|w'(e^{i\theta})|}{2\pi}\,d\theta.}
$$
The angular lift explains rigorously the derivative appearing in the printed hint despite the principal-angle branch cut.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By <Brownian scaling>, $r^{-1}Z_{r^2t}$ is planar Brownian motion started at $w_0/r$. Its unit-disk exit angle is exactly the original radius-$r$ exit angle. Substitute $z_0=w_0/r$ into the supplied <Poisson kernel for the unit disk>:
$$
\boxed{P^{w_0}(\theta_{T_{D_r}}\in d\theta)
=\frac{r^2-|w_0|^2}{2\pi|w_0-r e^{i\theta}|^2}\,d\theta.}
$$
This density is with respect to angle $d\theta$. Its density with respect to <arc length> would be smaller by a factor $r$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Put $\rho=|z|$. The <triangle inequality> gives $1-\rho\leq|z-e^{i\theta}|\leq1+\rho$, so the <Poisson kernel for the unit disk> obeys
$$
\frac{1-\rho}{1+\rho}\leq
\frac{1-|z|^2}{|z-e^{i\theta}|^2}
\leq\frac{1+\rho}{1-\rho}.
$$
If $\rho\leq r$, replace the lower bound by $(1-r)/(1+r)$ and the upper bound by $(1+r)/(1-r)$. Integrating against the positive measure $h(e^{i\theta})d\theta/(2\pi)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{1-r}{1+r}u(0)\leq u(z)\leq\frac{1+r}{1-r}u(0).}
$$
Here $u(0)$ is exactly the uniform boundary average from part (a). Positivity permits multiplication of the pointwise kernel bounds; continuity on the compact circle ensures all integrals are finite. This is the <Harnack inequality on the unit disk>.

= 6
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Use first that <planar Brownian motion avoids a fixed point>, so the path starting at $1$ never reaches zero. Its continuous logarithmic lift, initialized at zero, has real part $U_t=\log|Z_t|$ and imaginary part the continuous unwrapped angle $\Theta_t$. Local branches of the complex logarithm, patched along the path, and the <Itô formula> show that both coordinates are <continuous local martingales> with
$$
\langle U\rangle_t=\langle\Theta\rangle_t=\int_0^t\frac{ds}{|Z_s|^2}=:\tau(t),\qquad
\langle U,\Theta\rangle_t=0.
$$
The clock is finite on every compact time interval, since a continuous nonvanishing path stays a positive distance from zero there. It is continuous and strictly increasing. Moreover $\tau(\infty)=\infty$: by recurrence, planar Brownian motion spends infinite total time in a fixed disk, and inside the unit disk the clock integrand is at least one. This is the <divergence of a positive planar Brownian occupation integral>.

The <common-clock time change of orthogonal local martingales>, using the <Lévy characterization of multidimensional Brownian motion>, supplies independent Brownian coordinates $\zeta^1,\zeta^2$ such that $U_t+i\Theta_t=\zeta_{\tau(t)}$. Exponentiating gives
$$
\boxed{Z_t=\exp(\zeta_{\tau(t)}),\qquad\zeta_0=0.}
$$
To identify the inverse clock, write $A=\tau^{-1}$. The derivative of an inverse function and the representation just obtained give
$$
A'(s)=|Z_{A(s)}|^2=e^{2\zeta_s^1},\qquad
A(s)=\int_0^s e^{2\zeta_u^1}du.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\tau(t)=\int_0^t|Z_s|^{-2}ds
=\inf\left\{s\geq0:\int_0^s e^{2\zeta_u^1}du>t\right\}.}
$$
The strict inequality in this generalized inverse has the same infimum as the unique inverse value, since $A$ is continuous and strictly increasing onto the half-line.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Assume $0<r<1<R$, as required for the annulus and logarithms. The <logarithmic radius of planar Brownian motion> is a local martingale. Stopping at $T=T_{r,R}$ keeps it between the constants $\log r$ and $\log R$, so the stopped process is a bounded <martingale>. The exit time is finite almost surely because the annulus is bounded and Brownian motion eventually exits its containing disk.

The <optional stopping theorem> followed by <dominated convergence> gives $\mathbb E\log|Z_T|=\log|Z_0|=0$. Continuity puts the exit radius at either $r$ or $R$. If $p$ is the inner-exit probability, then $p\log r+(1-p)\log R=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{p=\frac{\log R}{\log R-\log r}.}
$$
Stopping before taking expectations matters: the unstopped logarithmic-radius process is a <strict local martingale>, so one cannot assume its unrestricted expectation is constant.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The second coordinate $\zeta^2$ is one-dimensional <Brownian motion>, whose zero set is unbounded almost surely by recurrence. The clock $\tau$ and its inverse are continuous, increasing, and unbounded, so the unwrapped angle $\Theta_t=\zeta^2_{\tau(t)}$ has zeros at arbitrarily large original times.

To ensure the times are <stopping times> for the original Brownian filtration, define directly
$$
T_0=0,\qquad T_n=\inf\{t\geq T_{n-1}+1:\Theta_t=0\}.
$$
The logarithmic lift makes $\Theta$ a continuous adapted process. Hitting a closed set after a stopping time is a stopping time under the usual filtration conditions, and the unbounded zero set proves inductively that each $T_n$ is finite almost surely. Since $T_n\geq T_{n-1}+1$, the sequence tends to infinity, and continuity gives
$$
\boxed{\zeta^2_{\tau(T_n)}=\Theta_{T_n}=0\quad\text{for every }n.}
$$
Geometrically, the Brownian path returns to the positive real ray at arbitrarily late times with **zero net unwrapped winding angle** about the origin. The assertion is stronger than principal argument zero, which also allows any integer number of completed turns.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

The long-time <Spitzer winding theorem> states that, for the continuous unwrapped angle $\Theta_t$ of planar <Brownian motion> started at $1$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{2\Theta_t}{\log t}\ \xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{\mathrm d}\ C,\qquad
f_C(x)=\frac1{\pi(1+x^2)}.}
$$
Here $C$ has the standard symmetric <Cauchy distribution>. The notation means <convergence in distribution>: for every real $x$,
$$
\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb P\left(\frac{2\Theta_t}{\log t}\leq x\right)
=\frac12+\frac1\pi\arctan x.
$$
Equivalently, expectations converge for every bounded continuous test function. This is a statement about the laws at large deterministic times, without asserting almost-sure convergence of sample paths. The angle is unwrapped; a principal argument is bounded and would instead give zero after division by $\log t$.
